= Introduction <sec:intro>

Locks remain the most common way systems software serializes access to shared state. The fairness they usually promise is FIFO acquisition: waiters receive the lock in the order they asked for it. Patel et al. showed that this promise is not enough @patel2020scl. When threads hold a contended lock for different lengths of time and spend much of their time holding it, the lock, not the CPU scheduler, decides how processor time is divided: each thread's share follows its hold time, whatever the scheduler intends. They called this _scheduler subversion_ and proposed _lock usage fairness_, which divides lock hold time rather than acquisitions in proportion to each thread's scheduling weight. In UpScaleDB @upscaledb, an embedded key-value store, they found that insert threads received nearly six times the CPU time of find threads.

Usage-fair locks solve this problem. Patel et al.’s u-SCL enforces usage fairness with two mechanisms @patel2020scl. It grants ownership in _lock slices_ of a few milliseconds, and it tracks each thread's lock usage and _bans_ a thread whose usage exceeds its share, keeping it from acquiring the lock until the other active threads have received sufficient lock time. A slice is dedicated to its owner: the owner may release and reacquire the lock freely, and no other thread may acquire it until the slice expires, even while the owner is outside its critical section. u-SCL is therefore not _work-conserving_: the lock can sit idle while other threads wait. CFL (Completely Fair Locking) removes this limitation @park2024cfl. It applies the policy of Linux's Completely Fair Scheduler to locks: just as CFS runs the task with the smallest virtual runtime, CFL tracks a priority-weighted _virtual lock hold time_ per thread and reorders the wait queue so that the waiter with the smallest value acquires next. Because it only reorders waiters and never bans them, the lock is never idle while a thread waits.

Both locks, however, keep the way locks have always worked: a thread that acquires the lock runs its own critical section, on its own core. Choosing who is served therefore also chooses where the critical section runs, and this coupling creates a dilemma between fairness and locality. A lock's performance depends heavily on locality: a critical section runs fastest when the protected data is already in the running core's cache, which is the case when the lock's previous holder ran on the same core. A hand-off to a thread on another core instead forces the data to migrate, one cache-to-cache transfer for every line the critical section touches. Chiplet processors, which split a socket into dies with private last-level caches @naffziger2021chiplet, make transfers between dies costlier still. #todo[cite or measure cross-die vs. same-die transfer latency, e.g. AMD EPYC CCD-to-CCD vs. intra-CCX] Fairness, however, forces hand-offs. Locks built for speed therefore choose locality. `parking_lot` @parkinglot, a widely used Rust library of compact, fast synchronization primitives, provides a `Mutex` that lets a releasing thread take the lock straight back and forces a hand-off to the longest waiter only about every 0.5 ms, while its strictly FIFO `FairMutex` is documented as "generally slower, but sometimes needed".

Neither u-SCL nor CFL resolves this dilemma; each gives up one side of it. u-SCL keeps locality by reserving the lock for its slice owner, and so leaves the lock idle while others wait. CFL keeps the lock busy and fair, and so hands it from core to core, moving the data with it. Can a lock have both, fairness without idleness and without moving the data? _Delegation_ locks suggest it can, because they remove the coupling behind the dilemma. In flat combining @hendler2010fc, threads publish their critical sections as requests and a single _combiner_ executes them on its own core, so the protected data stays in one cache no matter whose request runs. Existing delegation locks, from flat combining to TCLocks @gupta2023tclocks, use this separation for speed but serve requests in arrival order, and so remain as usage-unfair as FIFO locks. Yet once execution no longer follows the lock, choosing whose request runs next becomes a pure scheduling decision.

We build on this observation with FC-PQ, a usage-fair delegation lock whose combiner always serves the pending request of the thread that has received the least critical-section time, as in fair queueing @demers1989wfq. The combiner executes every request on its own core, keeping the protected data in one cache, and runs whenever a request is pending, so the lock never sits idle. FC-PQ thus obtains usage fairness without paying either cost above, and a thread that finds the lock uncontended runs its critical section directly. To predict when this pays, we develop an analysis framework that splits the price of fairness into accounting, migration, idleness, and redistribution; delegation removes migration and the idleness of reserving the lock, and leaves accounting and redistribution.

We evaluate FC-PQ in two embedded databases, redb @redb and UpScaleDB. In redb, FC-PQ raises the fairness of lock usage from 0.85 under the FIFO MCS lock @mellorcrummey1991mcs to 0.94--0.995 (Jain's index @jain1984fairness), close to u-SCL's, while matching the throughput of redb's built-in lock and moving five to twelve times fewer cache lines between cores than MCS. redb's writers issue transactions back to back, so u-SCL's reserved slices never sit idle, and FC-PQ only ties it. We expect FC-PQ to pull ahead when threads do other work between critical sections or the protected data is large, and @sec:discussion states these predictions.

This paper makes the following contributions:

- We show that the conventional lock design, in which the served thread runs its own critical section, couples who is served with where the critical section runs, and that this coupling makes a usage-fair lock pay in migration or, if it reserves the lock, in idleness.
- We give an analysis framework that decomposes the price of fairness and predicts when delegation lowers it.
- We design FC-PQ, a work-conserving, usage-fair delegation lock with a fast path proven sound for the uncontended case.
- We evaluate FC-PQ in redb and UpScaleDB with service charged to the requester and a fixed CPU clock, and report which costs of fairness delegation removes and which remain.

Section 2 reviews lock usage fairness; Section 3 develops the analysis framework; Section 4 presents FC-PQ; Section 5 describes our methodology; Sections 6 and 7 evaluate redb and UpScaleDB; Section 8 discusses limitations and predictions; Section 9 covers related work.
